WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:08.430
Sometimes things happen during post-production
that result in a complete film never being

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.430
released.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:13.530
Studios lose a few million, actors lose a
hard-earned credit on their résumé, and

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.289
the world is deprived of a potential cinematic
masterpiece.

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:21.699
Here are some films that are out there, somewhere,
but have never been officially unleashed.

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:22.999
The Fantastic Four

00:00:22.999 --> 00:00:27.539
Before a cinematic universe was even a gleam
in Marvel's eye, the company raised money

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:32.040
by selling off the film rights to its characters,
including the Fantastic Four.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:39.010
German production company Constantin Film
paid $250,000 for the FF rights in 1983—and

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.629
sat on them until 1992.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:45.400
Faced with the agreement's expiration and
needing to actually film something to retain

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.109
their rights, they slapped together a low-budget
movie in under a month.

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:54.019
Fantastic Four never hit theaters, as Marvel
bought back the rights for a few million and

00:00:54.019 --> 00:00:56.719
demanded every copy's destruction.

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:00.679
One leaked, however, and quickly became one
of the more legendary bootlegs appearing at

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:02.290
comic conventions.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.609
Big Bug Man

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:08.410
Big Bug Man is the last movie Marlon Brando
ever made, and it’s never been seen.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:13.800
In this adult animated superhero comedy, Brando
plays the villain, "Mrs. Sour," an evil old

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:14.800
lady.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.830
All of Brando's lines were recorded in his
home, where the ailing thespian insisted on

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.540
being dressed as an actual old lady, including
makeup.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.670
It sounds pretty nutty, but we still hope
to see this one some day.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.610
Especially since Brando told the crew that
it was the most fun he'd had making a movie

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.570
since 1953's Julius Caesar.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:33.570
Dark Blood

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:38.200
River Phoenix plays a man named Boy who lives
in the middle of a desert and becomes fixated

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.420
on a married woman.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.410
There's a kidnapping, an axe fight with a
jealous husband, and a whole lot of shots

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:44.410
of the desert.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:49.110
Phoenix died in 1993 with a few scenes left
unfilmed, although director George Sluizer

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.920
cobbled together a final cut almost 20 years
later.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.500
It’s never been seen outside of a few film
festivals.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.710
Gods Behaving Badly

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:01.170
Based on a comic novel about gods living in
modern day New York City, Gods Behaving Badly

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:06.730
features an all-star cast that includes Phylicia
Rashad as Demeter and John Turturro as Hades.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.840
Some of the only things that have surfaced
from this film are a bit of the title sequence

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:16.000
and some pictures of the cast, notably Christopher
Walken, standing next to Turturro, wearing

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.340
a ridiculous curly wig and a gold headband.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:23.681
It's hard to find a reason why this film never
made it past one film festival in Italy, but

00:02:23.681 --> 00:02:27.560
The Hollywood Reporter compared it to a “bad
1990s SNL sketch.”

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.560
Ouch.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:30.740
The Day the Clown Cried

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.750
“Holocaust drama” isn’t the first thing
that comes to mind when you think of Jerry

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.780
Lewis, but that’s the premise of The Day
the Clown Cried, directed by and starring

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.230
the slapstick comedian.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:46.310
Lewis plays a washed-up German circus clown
named Helmut Dork who’s forced by the Nazis

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.660
to trick children into the gas chamber.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.060
Lewis has repeatedly vowed that the film will
never be released.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.540
He claims that it’s because the rights to
the screenplay were never properly secured.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.910
But those who have seen it claim it’s because
it’s a disaster.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.760
Harry Shearer, best known for his voice work
on The Simpsons, is one of the few who’s

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.760
seen it.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:09.070
He’s called the film "drastically wrong"
and remarked that it's even worse than anyone

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:10.070
could imagine.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:16.550
“If you say Jerry Lewis clown in a concentration
camp and you make that movie up in your head,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:20.310
right, it’s so much better than that, by
better I mean worse.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.600
If you happen to actually see it, there's
really only one appropriate response.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.270
“What the hell was that”

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.690
Thanks for watching!

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:32.849
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:36.650
And leave us a comment to let us know which
of these lost movies you’d most like to

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:36.870
see...

